{
    "suite": "cose-x509-messages",
    "description": "Signed messages that carry what neither implementation will construct: a certificate thumbprint labelled with an algorithm that is not a hash algorithm (§24), and a certificate written down in an encoding that is not DER (§25). Generated by tools/X509Cases from the noble primitives alone; the chain and the signing key come from cose-x509-corpus.json, and each family's control is byte for byte a message both implementations produce.",
    "cases": [
        {
            "id": "thumbprint-labelled-with-sha-256",
            "class": "normative",
            "message": "",
            "trustAnchors": [
                "metrologyRoot"
            ],
            "expected": "accept",
            "thumbprintAlgorithm": "-16 (SHA-256)",
            "computedBy": "tools/X509Cases",
            "description": "The control, and the one of these four messages that neither implementation refuses: the x5t header names SHA-256, the hash algorithm the digest beside it was computed with. Its bytes are not this generator's word for what a sound message looks like — they are byte for byte the message both implementations produce and agree on for `cose-x509:thumbprint-names-the-certificate`, and the generator refuses to write this file unless it reproduces them. Without this row the three refusals beside it would prove nothing: a message that verifies for nobody is refused by everybody, whatever they check."
        },
        {
            "id": "thumbprint-labelled-with-a-signature-algorithm",
            "class": "normative",
            "message": "",
            "trustAnchors": [
                "metrologyRoot"
            ],
            "expected": "reject",
            "reason": "x5t carries a hash, and ES256 is a signature algorithm, not a hash algorithm",
            "thumbprintAlgorithm": "-7 (ES256)",
            "computedBy": "tools/X509Cases",
            "description": "The same message, with one CBOR integer changed: the x5t header labels the thumbprint ES256. The digest beside it is still the correct SHA-256 of the end-entity certificate, so an implementation that reads the digest and computes SHA-256 without looking at the label finds a match and verifies the message — which is what makes refusing it a measurable answer rather than the only possible one. ES256 names SHA-256 for the signature it makes, and RFC 9360 §2 gives x5t a hash; a thumbprint that named ES256 would claim a computation nobody performed. No error text is judged: failing to read the message counts as refusing it, and verifying it is the one wrong answer."
        },
        {
            "id": "thumbprint-labelled-with-a-mac-algorithm",
            "class": "normative",
            "message": "",
            "trustAnchors": [
                "metrologyRoot"
            ],
            "expected": "reject",
            "reason": "x5t carries a hash, and HMAC 256/256 is a MAC algorithm, not a hash algorithm",
            "thumbprintAlgorithm": "5 (HMAC 256/256)",
            "computedBy": "tools/X509Cases",
            "description": "The same message again, labelling the thumbprint HMAC 256/256. It stands beside the ES256 row rather than duplicating it: HMAC 256/256 also names SHA-256, for the tag it computes, so an implementation that refuses only signature algorithms passes the row before this one and fails here. The digest is again the correct SHA-256 of the end-entity certificate."
        },
        {
            "id": "thumbprint-labelled-with-a-key-derivation-algorithm",
            "class": "normative",
            "message": "",
            "trustAnchors": [
                "metrologyRoot"
            ],
            "expected": "reject",
            "reason": "x5t carries a hash, and ECDH-ES + HKDF-256 is a key agreement algorithm, not a hash algorithm",
            "thumbprintAlgorithm": "-25 (ECDH-ES + HKDF-256)",
            "computedBy": "tools/X509Cases",
            "description": "The third of the same message, labelling the thumbprint ECDH-ES + HKDF-256. It names SHA-256 for the key derivation it performs, so the three rows together separate an implementation that asks whether an algorithm has a digest — which all three do — from one that asks whether it is a hash algorithm, which is what RFC 9054 registers and what both implementations check. The first kind verifies all three messages."
        },
        {
            "id": "the-certificate-as-it-was-minted",
            "class": "normative",
            "message": "",
            "trustAnchors": [
                "metrologyRoot"
            ],
            "expected": "accept",
            "encoding": "both certificates as tools/CertificateCorpus minted them",
            "computedBy": "tools/X509Cases",
            "description": "The control of this family, on the same argument as the thumbprint control above: its bytes are byte for byte the message both implementations produce and agree on for `cose-x509:chain-to-trusted-root`, and the generator refuses to write this file unless it reproduces them. So the refusals below are refusals of a sound message with one certificate written down differently, and of nothing else — not of a construction this generator got wrong."
        },
        {
            "id": "a-leading-zero-in-the-outer-length",
            "class": "normative",
            "message": "",
            "trustAnchors": [
                "metrologyRoot"
            ],
            "expected": "reject",
            "reason": "the certificate's outer length is `83 00 01 4E`, three octets where two suffice, and DER writes a length in the fewest octets that hold it",
            "encoding": "the certificate at position 0 of the chain, with a leading zero in its outer length",
            "computedBy": "tools/X509Cases",
            "description": "The end-entity certificate, written down with a length that is not DER. The tampering is outside the TBSCertificate, so the issuer's signature over it still holds and the chain still reaches the anchor: an implementation that does not check the encoding verifies this message. Bouncy Castle read it before Styx f1057ea8; COSE.TS refuses it at the length octets."
        },
        {
            "id": "an-indefinite-outer-length",
            "class": "normative",
            "message": "",
            "trustAnchors": [
                "metrologyRoot"
            ],
            "expected": "reject",
            "reason": "the certificate is wrapped in `30 80` … `00 00`, which is BER and not DER, and RFC 5280 §4.1 requires DER",
            "encoding": "the certificate at position 0 of the chain, with an indefinite outer length",
            "computedBy": "tools/X509Cases",
            "description": "The same certificate with an indefinite length, which is the one tampering here that a reader cannot start accepting by leaving a check out — it has to implement BER to get through it at all. That this row opens is therefore not argued from a mutilated reader but measured on a shipped one: Bouncy Castle read this certificate, and Styx verified this message, before f1057ea8."
        },
        {
            "id": "one-byte-after-the-certificate",
            "class": "normative",
            "message": "",
            "trustAnchors": [
                "metrologyRoot"
            ],
            "expected": "reject",
            "reason": "one octet follows the certificate inside the x5chain byte string, and an x5chain entry carries one certificate and nothing else",
            "encoding": "the certificate at position 0 of the chain, with one byte appended after it",
            "computedBy": "tools/X509Cases",
            "description": "The certificate followed by a byte that is not part of it. A parser that reads the first certificate and stops — which is what `X509CertificateParser.ReadCertificate` does — never sees that byte, so this is the row that asks whether what arrived was read to its end. COSE.TS insists on it in `DerReader.expectEnd`."
        },
        {
            "id": "a-long-form-length-inside-the-certificate",
            "class": "normative",
            "message": "",
            "trustAnchors": [
                "metrologyRoot"
            ],
            "expected": "reject",
            "reason": "the signatureAlgorithm's length is `81 0A` where `0A` suffices, so a length inside the certificate is not DER although the outer one is",
            "encoding": "the certificate at position 0 of the chain, with a long-form length in its signatureAlgorithm",
            "computedBy": "tools/X509Cases",
            "description": "The length that is not DER sits inside the certificate rather than in its outer header, which separates a reader that validates the record it was handed from one that validates every record in it: the first passes the leading-zero row above and fails this one. The outer length is recomputed, so the certificate is well formed apart from that single field."
        },
        {
            "id": "one-byte-after-the-second-certificate",
            "class": "normative",
            "message": "",
            "trustAnchors": [
                "metrologyRoot"
            ],
            "expected": "reject",
            "reason": "one octet follows the certification authority's certificate, at position 1 of the chain, and every entry of an x5chain carries one certificate",
            "encoding": "the certificate at position 1 of the chain, with one byte appended after it",
            "computedBy": "tools/X509Cases",
            "description": "The same appended byte, one position further up the chain. An implementation that checks the encoding of the certificate a message is about and then parses the rest of the chain however it can passes every row above and verifies this one. Styx f1057ea8 names the position in its refusal for that reason."
        },
        {
            "id": "not-der-with-a-thumbprint-over-what-travelled",
            "class": "normative",
            "message": "",
            "trustAnchors": [
                "metrologyRoot"
            ],
            "expected": "reject",
            "reason": "the certificate is not DER, which is reason enough before any thumbprint is compared — and this thumbprint is the honest hash of the bytes that travelled",
            "encoding": "the certificate at position 0 of the chain, with a leading zero in its outer length",
            "thumbprintOver": "the bytes in the message",
            "computedBy": "tools/X509Cases",
            "description": "The second question §25 asked. x5t is the hash of a certificate's DER encoding [RFC 9360, Section 2], so when a parser accepts an encoding that is not DER, which bytes does it hash? Here the thumbprint is the hash of exactly what is in the message, so an implementation that reads the certificate leniently and hashes what it was given finds a match and verifies — COSE.TS hashes the record as it appeared, and would. The tampering is the leading zero rather than the indefinite length precisely so that this row has a demonstrated opening and not an argued one."
        },
        {
            "id": "not-der-with-a-thumbprint-over-the-re-encoding",
            "class": "normative",
            "message": "",
            "trustAnchors": [
                "metrologyRoot"
            ],
            "expected": "reject",
            "reason": "the certificate is not DER, and the thumbprint names its canonical encoding, which is not what travelled",
            "encoding": "the certificate at position 0 of the chain, with a leading zero in its outer length",
            "thumbprintOver": "the canonical DER, which is not in the message",
            "computedBy": "tools/X509Cases",
            "description": "The same certificate, with x5t over the canonical DER of it — a byte string that is not in this message and that the sender never sent. An implementation that reads the certificate leniently and hashes its own re-encoding finds a match here and verifies: that is Styx before f1057ea8, where `GetEncoded()` returned Bouncy Castle's encoding rather than the bytes read. Together with the row before it, this pins the refusal to the certificate: a lenient reader hashes either what it got or what it re-encoded, and each of those two verifies one of these two rows, so both green means the certificate itself was refused."
        }
    ]
}
